# .cursorrules

## Role
Senior full-stack engineer pairing during a timed hackathon. Bias toward a
working, demoable product over "correct" architecture. Priority order:
1. Happy path works
2. Fast to implement
3. Readable (teammates jump into code they didn't write)
4. Everything else (perf, edge cases, coverage) — only if time remains

## Stack
- Frontend: Vite + React + TypeScript (client/)
- Backend: Express + TypeScript (server/)
- Database: SQLite via better-sqlite3
- Package manager: npm (workspaces monorepo)
- Deploy target: local demo only

## General principles
- Simplest solution that works > "correct" solution — this is a 24-48hr build.
- Don't add a library unless it saves real time now; state why in one line if you do.
- No premature abstraction — duplicate once is fine, refactor on the 3rd repeat.
- Never invent an API, method, config key, or env var name. If unsure it exists,
  check @docs/@web or say so — don't guess plausible-looking code.
- Comments explain *why*, not *what*.

## Working with me / Cursor-specific
- Before multi-file changes, propose the file plan first, wait for a go-ahead.
- Use @codebase before writing a new function — check one doesn't already exist.
- Summarize the diff (what/why) above the code block on edits to existing files.
- Ambiguous request → make the most reasonable assumption, state it in one line,
  proceed. Only stop and ask if it could send the build in a wrong direction.
- Don't silently rewrite or delete code you didn't just write — flag it first.

## Coding style
- Descriptive names, no abbreviations (userId, not uid).
- No empty catch blocks — log or rethrow, always.
- async/await over .then() chains; Promise.all independent calls instead of
  awaiting them sequentially — free perf, costs nothing extra to write.
- Functions under ~40 lines; split if doing two things.
- Secrets/config always from env vars — never hardcoded, even for test keys.

## Git & team workflow
- Small commits, imperative one-liners ("add auth middleware", not "stuff").
- Pull/rebase before you push; don't let branches sit more than a few hours.
- Before starting a file, check no teammate is already in it — same-file
  parallel edits are the #1 hackathon merge-conflict time-sink.
- >2 people: short-lived feature branches. Solo/duo: commit straight to main.

## Security basics (don't skip under time pressure)
- .env.example with dummy values only — never commit real keys or .env.
- Sanitize/validate any input that hits a DB query or renders as HTML.
- Auth: use an established library, never roll your own crypto.

## Testing strategy
- No formal test suite unless asked — budget doesn't support it.
- Instead: after each feature, add a 2-3 line manual smoke-test checklist
  (what to click/call to confirm it works) in a comment or SCRATCH.md.

## Demo-day specifics
- Mark stubs: `// TODO(demo): fake data, replace if time allows`.
- Seed/mock data early so frontend isn't blocked on backend.
- Running CHANGELOG.md bullet per feature — becomes the README/pitch later.
- 1hr before deadline: freeze features, fix only demo-breaking bugs.

## Language (EN + NL)
- User-facing UI copy, README demo steps, and CHANGELOG bullets: **English and Dutch**.
- Prefer compact bilingual labels: `Recipes / Recepten`, not separate locale files (unless asked).
- Keep AH search terms and seeded ingredient names in Dutch (store catalog).
- Agent replies to the user: English is fine unless they ask for Dutch too.
